\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why architecture matters to post-training potential}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{language-model}
\tag{transformer}

\p{The definition in \ref{ftip-0003} separates a stable semantic interface
from its implementation. This is a consensus move across probabilistic
language modelling, Transformer architecture, and reinforcement-learning
work: downstream protocols consume token probabilities, while their rates and
failure modes depend on hidden-state geometry, normalization, routing, memory,
and the output head.}

\p{The following diagram is \strong{schematic}, not a claim that every current
model uses a dense Transformer. It marks the interfaces at which later FTIP
theorems may place assumptions.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  node distance=7mm,
  every node/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center,
    text width=25mm, minimum height=10mm},
  every path/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node (tok) {token prefix $x_{\leq t}$};
\node[right=of tok] (emb) {embedding\\and position};
\node[right=of emb] (stack) {causal stack:\\attention, MLP,\\routing or recurrence};
\node[right=of stack] (head) {normalization\\and output head};
\node[right=of head] (dist) {next-token law $\pi_\theta$};
\draw (tok) -- (emb);
\draw (emb) -- (stack);
\draw (stack) -- (head);
\draw (head) -- (dist);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Architecture can affect potential in at least three ways. It changes the
base distribution created per unit of pretraining compute; it changes the
Jacobian and gradient geometry through which post-training signals propagate;
and it changes the cost of generating long agentic rollouts. Parameter count
alone therefore cannot be the architecture coordinate of a scaling law.}

\p{The first formal questions are interface questions: when does a causal mask
give prefix non-interference; which output-head or normalization assumptions
justify gradient-energy bounds; and how do sparse routing or recurrent state
alter update stability? These questions draw on architecture papers, but the
post-training statements themselves belong to FTIP rather than to a basic
Transformer formalization.}
